      "snippet": {
        "text": "Compared to teacher self-efficacy, the relationship between teachers' emotions and job satisfaction has been explored with the relatively recent development of measures to assess discrete teacher emotions (e.g. Chen, 2016;Frenzel et al., 2016). With a focus on examining emotional exhaustion (EE) among secondary school teachers, Keller et al. (2014) used the experience sampling method with 39 German teachers and generated over seven hundred emotional experiences in terms of enjoyment, anxiety, anger, and two momentary emotional labour (EL) indicators (faking and suppression). They found EL was induced by only anger. Comparatively, they found teachers' enjoyment beneficial for reducing EL, leading to decreased burnout. In a validation study to develop the Teacher Emotions Scales (TES) with over nine hundred primary and secondary school teachers, Frenzel et al. (2016) found good psychometrical properties to support the TES with three discrete emotions (enjoyment, anger, and anxiety). These emotions also have 'theoretically meaningful relations with teachers' general affect, burnout, job satisfaction, and teacher self-efficacy ' (p. 148). With 564 Turkish preschool to high school teachers, Atmaca et al. (2020) found joy and love to be positively correlated with job satisfaction, while the stress dimension significantly predicted teachers' burnout. Based on the PERMA framework in positive psychology, Dreer (2021) found over five hundred primary and secondary school teachers' PERMA profiles (positive emotions, engagement, relationships, meaning, and achievement) positively linked to their job satisfaction. Among PERMA, positive emotions showed the strongest predictive effects on job satisfaction, suggesting the salient role of positive emotions in German teachers' wellbeing. Huang et al. (2020) examined the effects of informal teaching-learning (ITL) with 2,880 primary school teachers in mainland China. Huang et al. (2020) found a positive association between teachers' enjoyment and engagement in five types of ITL (i.e. self-reflection, student interaction, colleague interaction, stakeholder interaction, and learning through media). In a study by Wang et al. (2022) among 947 Canadian practicing teachers, these scholars found important implications of three coping profiles for teachers' emotions, job satisfaction, and quitting intentions.",
