        "openAccessInfo": {
          "license": "CCBYNCND",
          "status": "GOLD",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10660036, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Teaching is a demanding career characterized by high attrition rate [39]. A plethora of research suggests that teachers are prune to stress, low self-esteem and efficacy, and weak job satisfaction, often reducing their wellbeing, and hence leading them to burning out [19,40]. Thus, teacher wellbeing is integral for teachers because it is a premise for securing job satisfaction, positive emotions, professional growth, reduced dropout, and successful outcomes for learners [41][42][43]. Moreover, teacher wellbeing often support them in managing the demands of their career, and making them live a good life [44,45]. \n\nTeacher wellbeing is multidimensional and holistic [46] encompassing overlapping and interrelated dimensions that are physical, social, emotional and psychological [47][48][49][50][51][52][53][54][55]. In fact, research suggests that a limited teacher agency tend to reduce teacher wellbeing, impacting negatively on teacher performance, teaching effectiveness, student outcomes, school improvement, and school effectiveness [53,55,56]. \n\nHow teacher wellbeing is safeguarded in the literature is demonstrated through a myriad of factors. However, according to this literature, school climate is the determinant factor in shaping the provision of teacher wellbeing in schools [51]. Positive school climates serve as critical factors in decreased teacher absenteeism, and increased sense of belonging to school [51,53]. Moreover, in a climate of collaboration and congeniality, teachers tend to feel supported from burning out, leading to improved engagement, performance, and student learning outcomes [54,55]. Finally, the establishment and nourishment of teacher leadership construct in schools, is regarded as an effective means for addressing teachers' wellbeing [12,57,58]. \n\nFinally, poor teacher wellbeing has significant repercussions on both learning and teaching. Ref. [59] shed light on this issue by highlighting the occupational nature of teacher wellbeing, which encompasses cognitive, emotional, health, and social aspects related to their work and profession.",
        "snippetKind": "body",
        "section": "Teacher wellbeing",
