      "snippet": {
        "text": "career teachers often face heightened stress levels and attrition rates 1,19,20 . The first five years of service are critical for early career teachers, who may lack the fully developed ability to appraise their workplace, unlike their more experienced colleagues 13,21 . Research findings on the influence of age on teacher wellbeing suggest that younger teachers are more likely to leave the profession compared to middle-aged teachers 22 . Professional development and qualifications also influence teacher wellbeing. Teachers with more exposure to professional development have shown higher job satisfaction, particularly for female teachers with high self-efficacy 23 . Highly qualified female teachers are less likely to leave the teaching profession, whereas male teachers' attrition is not influenced by qualification 24 . \n\nIn the context of school conditions and characteristics, the school environment significantly impacts teacher experiences and wellbeing. Positive school climates, supportive leadership, and collaborative teacher relationships enhance job satisfaction and overall wellbeing 25 . Conversely, negative school environments marked by inadequate resources, excessive workload, and limited professional growth opportunities, can lead to burnout and decreased job satisfaction 26 . Schools that offer administrative support, mentoring programs for early-career teachers, and opportunities for networking and cooperation tend to have lower attrition rates 25 . Additionally, class size and composition can impact teachers' job satisfaction 11 . Some studies have shown that public schools with high enrolments of economically disadvantaged students tend to have higher teacher turnover, while others have contested this claim 27,28 . \n\nAddressing the factors influencing teacher wellbeing is crucial for minimising stress, burnout, and attrition among educators. Understanding how teachers' demographic factors interact with the school environment is vital for developing targeted interventions to improve teacher wellbeing across different demographics. Additionally, as the school environment is multifaceted, analysing its individual components and their collective impact on teacher wellbeing requires a holistic approach 11 . Furthermore, evidence from past research suggests that primary school teachers seem to experience greater levels of stress and burnout compared to secondary school teachers as a result of the hours spent in meeting classroom demands which is higher compared to other teacher types 1,29,30 .",
