      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study aimed to validate the adapted OECD teacher wellbeing survey tool and evaluate the school and teacher conditions/characteristics that influence and shape the core dimensions of mainstream primary school teachers' occupational wellbeing. Teacher wellbeing is pivotal for a conducive learning environment and has continued to receive much attention 4 . The study provides validated evidence for the adapted teacher wellbeing tool based on its internal structure. The factor loadings for each item underscored the robustness of the measurement instrument, with items aligning well with their respective dimensions. The internal consistency assessments using Cronbach's alpha coefficients demonstrated good reliability for all wellbeing dimensions-cognitive, subjective, physical-mental and social dimensions. The positive correlations between the cognitive dimension and the subjective and social dimensions suggest that teachers with higher cognitive wellbeing also tended      dimensions provide insights into the complex relationships between cognitive, subjective, physical-mental, and social aspects of teachers' wellbeing 6,11 . \n\nThe results indicate that Australian primary school teachers generally exhibit good self-efficacy, positively linked to social support from colleagues and principals, leading to job satisfaction and lower psychosomatic symptoms. This study found that teachers demonstrated high self-efficacy in instruction, student engagement, and classroom management. Moreover, social support networks further enhanced teacher wellbeing. The teachers also derived job satisfaction from social support from colleagues and principals. Previous studies have reported that high teacher self-efficacy is associated with improved job satisfaction and performance, lower stress levels, and greater persistence 43 . Teachers experiencing psychosomatic symptoms reported low or lack of social support from colleagues and principals. The findings align with previous studies highlighting the psychosomatic challenges faced by teachers, emphasising the correlation with burnout syndrome, a leading cause of premature retirement 44 . Previous studies argued that teachers suffered more psychosomatic disorders than other occupational groups 45 . The study also underscores the importance of social support, especially from school leadership and colleagues, as a significant factor in teacher wellbeing. However, non-classroom teachers showed a significant negative decrease in social wellbeing compared to teachers from Prep classes. Positive social support correlates with higher self-efficacy and job satisfaction, while low social support is associated with poor mental health in teachers [46][47][48] .",
