      "snippet": {
        "text": "The pedagogical approaches commonly used by the educators in the classes that took part on the research, were either collaborative learning (Linden et al., 2000), project base or problem-based learning (Krajcik & Blumenfeld, 2005), or a combination of both. These pedagogies seem to fit well for designing AI literacy interventions (Ng et al., 2023). \n\nAs for framing the AI tools use on the tasks proposed to students, the Artificial Intelligence Assessment Scale (AIAS) (Perkins et al., 2024), that offers a structured, flexible approach for determining the appropriate level of AI usage, based on the learning outcomes of a given assessment, was considered. \n\nFor the development of the pre-test and post-test questionnaire, regarding the needs of skills and digital skills of students to be measured aiming for AI literacy, the Digital Competence Framework for Citizens (DigComp) was borrowed as a reference, specifically the DigComp 2.2 update (Vuorikari et al., 2022), which addresses the topic of citizens interacting with AI systems. \n\nA quasi-experimental pre-test, post-test research design, widely used in higher education research to evaluate the effectiveness of various educational interventions such as the implementation of a new technology or teaching method (Akçayır et al., 2016;Alt & Raichel, 2020), was conducted with an experimental group and a control group, in order to analyze the perception and competencies, before and after, of students on the use of Generative Artificial Intelligence tools (in particular with ChatGPT) in university subjects, with the aim of generating proposals about its use in class. Throughout the course, teachers encouraged students from the experimental group to use ChatGPT for targeted use without limitations (that is, AI use from levels 2 to 5 of the AIAS scale), whereas level 1 of AIAS (no AI assistance) (Perkins et al., 2024) was the norm for the control group.",
