      "snippet": {
        "text": "Before the experiment began, a pre-test was conducted using the Teacher Efficacy Scale and the Advanced Thinking Scale to collect baseline data from both the experimental and control groups [44]. During the fourweek experimental phase, both groups received training in teaching thinking and problem-solving skills, but only the experimental group integrated GAI-assisted instructional activities. Specifically, the experimental group engaged in tasks such as optimizing existing teaching plans with GAI, generating complete lesson plans on given topics, simulating teacher-student dialogues using GAI, and refining their instructional designs based on AI-generated feedback. These activities required them to critically evaluate AI outputs, adjust prompts, and reflect on cognitive strategies. In contrast, the control group followed traditional methods, including manual lesson planning, group discussions, textbook-based case analysis, and classroom simulations without AI tools. Both groups received equal training time and identical learning objectives, with the only variable being the use of generative AI in the experimental group. \n\nThe main tasks assigned to the experimental group over the month-long experiment include: Using GAI to optimize a given teaching design; In the second week, generating a teaching design for a specified topic using GAI and continuing teaching training with GAI.3. In the third week, simulating classroom exercises using GAI for the teacher-student interaction segment of the specified teaching design; In the fourth week, generating and optimizing teaching designs using GAI and conducting simulated classroom training. In contrast, the control group did not use GAI to assist the teaching scene, but adopted traditional classroom teaching methods and thinking methods.",
