      "snippet": {
        "text": "Figure 1 shows the initial hypothetical model in which there are four underlying constructs related to establishing three research hypotheses that are supported by the previous literature: \n\nThe mediating role of work stress in the relationship between workload and AI model usage among preservice teachers may be substantiated by several theoretical and empirical considerations. Excessive workload has been identified as a significant predictor of work stress in educational settings, potentially depleting teachers' mental and physical resources [3,26]. As preservice teachers experience mounting academic and professional demands, the resulting psychological pressure could influence their technological adoption decisions. Kim and Lee [23] suggested that while AI adoption does not directly cause burnout, it may impact psychological wellbeing through the mediating mechanism of job stress, indicating a complex relationship between technological integration and occupational strain. This dynamic might be particularly relevant for preservice teachers navigating both professional preparation demands and emerging technological requirements. Further research by Jeong et al. [22] indicates that work-related stress functions as a mediating variable between organizational conditions and technology utilization patterns, suggesting that stress levels could significantly influence how preservice teachers approach and implement AI tools. The cognitive and emotional burdens associated with excessive workload might alter risk perception, technology acceptance attitudes, and implementation behaviors regarding AI models [17]. Additionally, Quispe et al. [16] demonstrated that workload and perceived stress affect job satisfaction, which could subsequently influence technology adoption decisions among Peruvian educators. These interconnected relationships suggest that work stress may serve as a critical intervening mechanism through which workload affects preservice teachers' engagement with and implementation of AI models in educational contexts. Consequently, it is formulated as follows: \n\nHypothesis 1 Work stress (WS) significantly mediates the influence of workload (WL) on the use of AI models (USAI) by preservice teachers. The serial mediation hypothesis, which proposes that work stress and performance expectations jointly mediate the relationship between workload and AI model usage among preservice teachers, provides theoretical support for multiple strands of research. Excessive workload has been associated with increased work stress among educators, with studies indicating that high job demands deplete mental resources and lead to psychological strain [3,31].",
