      "snippet": {
        "text": "It proposes appropriate ethical guidelines for the use of AI in education. Furthermore, in their review of AIED K-12 ethics policy documents, Adams et al. (2023) found that the core AI ethical principles of Transparency; Justice and Fairness; Non-maleficence; Responsibility; Privacy; Beneficence; Freedom & Autonomy were present, and sometimes adapted for children. Four new ethical principles unique to K-12 education were also identified, including Pedagogical Appropriateness; Children's Rights; AI Literacy; and Teacher Wellbeing (Adams et al., 2023). \n\nWhile these ethical principles are relevant to inform and guide AIED deployment in K-12 education, there is still minimal research and significant ambiguity regarding how the ethics of AI use are conceptualised and operationalised in school classrooms worldwide by teachers and students alike and what the challenges are. This paper addresses this critical gap in the literature by investigating the key concerns relating to ethical AI use within the context of K-12 education. Providing a more nuanced understanding of the ethics of AI in schools is of particular significance as it can inform and drive future research and implementation of AI ethics curricula. More specifically, the paper reports on the findings of a systematic literature review of 25 peer-reviewed papers, which was guided by the following research questions: \n\n• What are the primary challenges relating to AI ethics encountered in the context of K-12 education? \n\n• What are the key responses relating to AI ethics encountered in the context of K-12 education?",
