          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1080/1475939X.2024.2428601?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1080/1475939X.2024.2428601, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "In this article, we have carried out a systematic literature review of emerging research in the ethics of AI in K-12 education. Our rigorous search of eight databases and the subsequent filtering processes identified 25 peer-reviewed studies that met our inclusion and exclusion criteria. Overall, this review highlighted the complexity of conceptualising and operationalising AI ethics in K-12 education and reported on the responses so far. Our findings revealed that embedding AI ethics courses in compulsory schooling is often informed by national imperatives and, in some instances, forms part of wider AI literacy programmes driven by neoliberal agendas relating to training a future AI-ready workforce. \n\nOne of the main challenges this SLR identified was students' and teachers' narrow understanding and misconceptions about ethical concerns underpinning AI use. A significant component relates to teachers' insufficient preparedness to support their students with developing AI ethics literacy. Given the speed with which AI technologies are becoming part of everyday life and education, this lack of awareness about AI ethics is highly concerning, albeit not surprising. As the long history of ed tech implementation has shown (Cuban, 1986), this is not the first instance when a digital tool has received heightened attention and intense promotion by industry and policy without critical consideration of the digital literacies and pedagogies that are also required for its meaningful and responsible adoption in educational settings. Indeed, the various challenges identified in this article also bring into light the disjuncture between topdown imperatives to embed AI in educational settings and teachers' and students' unfamiliarity with the ethical implications of AI use. \n\nHowever, in contrast to previous types of ed tech, which could be seen as somehow more benign, many of the issues surrounding the (mis) use of AI in education are intricately linked with broader issues of power, inequalities and exclusion (Verdegem, 2021). Given the complex and potentially troubling ethical ramifications of AI use, this SLR highlights the urgent need to develop relevant and age-appropriate approaches, curricula and pedagogies to support teachers and students with cultivating AI ethics literacy. Our results also indicate that technical knowledge alone is insufficient for grasping the complexity of AI ethical challenges.",
