        "text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) is pivotal in personalizing STEM education and enhancing student engagement and motivation. By leveraging AI-driven personalized learning algorithms, educators can tailor educational content to individual student preferences and abilities, thus keeping students engaged with material suited to their needs (Wadhwa et al., 2024;Ifraheem et al., 2024). AI tools like intelligent tutoring systems, adaptive learning platforms, and learning analytics contribute to reshaping educational outcomes and redefining student engagement, leading to positive recognition of AI's value in lifelong learning (Ifraheem et al., 2024). Additionally, AI facilitates advanced analytics, instructional automation, and adaptive testing in STEM education, offering innovative strategies to enhance learning outcomes and improve student engagement (Triplett, 2023). (Tiwari, 2023). The transformative potential of AI in education, particularly in personalized learning, improves student outcomes and fosters increased engagement and enriched educational experiences (Alam, 2021). \n\nArtificial Intelligence (AI) is transformative in STEM education, offering numerous benefits, including personalized learning, advanced analytics, and instructional automation. AI enhances students' knowledge through tailored learning experiences, improving performance across various subjects. Furthermore, it equips educators with tools to address educational challenges and improve overall learning experiences creatively. AI is instrumental in personalizing STEM education and boosting student engagement and motivation. Educators can customize educational content by utilizing AI-driven personalized learning algorithms to align with individual student preferences and abilities. AI tools such as intelligent tutoring systems, adaptive learning platforms, and learning analytics contribute to reshaping educational outcomes and redefining student engagement, fostering a positive recognition of AI's value in lifelong learning. \n\nHowever, integrating AI into education presents challenges, including ethical concerns, the need for increased AI literacy among stakeholders, and potential issues related to student engagement when AI assumes a predominant role in monitoring and controlling the learning process. Striking a balance between these benefits and limitations is crucial for maximizing the effectiveness of AI in STEM education. \n\nThe transformative potential of AI in education, particularly in personalized learning, improves student outcomes and fosters increased engagement and enriched educational experiences. As AI evolves, its impact on STEM education will likely grow, necessitating ongoing evaluation and adaptation to ensure optimal implementation and effectiveness.",
