      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) in education encompasses using AI technologies like chatbots, automated grading systems, intelligent tutoring systems, and platforms predicting student performance to bolster educational processes (Chiu et al., 2023a(Chiu et al., , 2023b;;Namaziandost & Rezai, 2024b). Research on AI predominantly explores the development of AI tools, examining the efficacy of learning algorithms, ethical considerations, and the fundamental rights of AI users (Berendt et al., 2020;Chiu et al., 2022;Cope et al., 2020;Luckin & Cukurova, 2019;McStay, 2020). A comprehensive review reveals that AI technologies are integrated into four primary educational areas: teaching, learning, assessment, and administration (Chiu et al., 2023a(Chiu et al., , 2023b)). \n\nIn recent years, applied linguists have paid close attention to how support provided by teachers might affect language learning. Based on the existing literature, teachers can support the learners' language learning process using AI tools (see Chiu et al., 2023aChiu et al., , 2023b)). Perceived teacher support refers to the belief among students that they can depend on their teachers for help (Hendrickx et al., 2016;Kim et al., 2018). Given students' significant time with teachers, this support is crucial for their academic growth (Lei et al., 2018). According to attachment theory, interactions with attachment figures are essential for human development (Bowlby, 1977). Children who consistently experience secure attachment tend to see themselves as valuable (Verschueren et al., 2012) and show more exploratory behaviors (Shaver & Mikulincer, 2002). Due to similarities between parent-child and teacher-student relationships, researchers suggest teachers can serve as \"ad hoc attachment figures\" for students (Pianta, 1999;Verschueren & Koomen, 2012), particularly for primary school students who are highly receptive to teacher support (Leflot et al., 2011).",
