      "snippet": {
        "text": "The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) in higher education presents a complex challenge for preservice teachers, who face significant academic and professional pressures. In the international context, recent research has shown that academic overload has a direct effect on recovery needs, especially under high student demands [1]. Preservice teachers experience increased levels of work stress when AI tools are incorporated into their pedagogical practices, a situation intensified by the limited technological preparation they receive during their training [2]. The scientific literature has established that AI adoption significantly increases work stress, leading to professional burnout among educators (Kim & Lee, 2024), whereas other studies have identified the mediating role of mental stress between work-family conflict and job satisfaction among teachers [3]. Concurrently, problematic AI use has been shown to generate adverse consequences, including increased technological dependency, reduced creativity, and diminished critical thinking [4,5]. Additionally, research in various educational contexts has demonstrated that information overload has a direct negative effect on teacher job performance, significantly impacting role-related stress, which deteriorates teaching quality [6]. \n\nDespite growing academic interest in educational AI, a significant knowledge gap persists regarding the interaction between academic overload, work stress, and performance expectations in the specific context of preservice teachers. Various investigations have identified a substantial gap between the theoretical recognition of AI's potential and its practical application in educational settings [7,8], highlighting the urgent need for higher education institutions to improve AI literacy among future teachers. This discrepancy is particularly relevant in initial teacher training, where there is a limited understanding of how academic pressures condition the adoption of emerging technologies [9]. Although recent studies have explored the relationship between academic self-efficacy and AI dependency, which is mediated by academic stress and performance expectations [10], specific research articulating these factors in Latin American educational contexts is necessary. Solórzano et al. [11] have demonstrated that habits and facilitating conditions significantly influence AI use among university students, whereas Acosta-Enriquez et al. [12] have established that performance expectancy and hedonic motivation are significant predictors of AI use in university settings via the UTAUT2 model. However, analysis of these factors in the particular context of initial teacher training is still needed.",
