        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Teacher well-being is a multifaceted concept that refers not just to teachers' overall physical, psychological and mental health; it also encompasses \"the ability of teachers to develop a positive though dynamic equilibrium between teachers' resources and their challenges/demands (environmental, social, individual, physical, mental, psychological)\" at school (Benevene et al., 2020, p. 1). Today, \"the complex and ever-changing nature of teachers' work challenges their well-being\" (Price & McCallum, 2015, p. 195). The stresses of teaching, including overwork, pressures to keep current, social isolation, workplace insecurity, burnout and/or lack of perceived agency can result in a diminishment of well-being (Allies, 2021;Turner et al., 2021). Teacher well-being is positively correlated with student success. For example, Turner et al. (2021) reported an increase in student accomplishment and learning when teachers perceived an increased positive outlook supported by mental health strategies. \n\nThe policy documents examined offered multiple cautions about the use of AI in schools in relation to teacher well-being including increased teacher workloads, the need for ongoing professional development, additional preparation time, possible diminishment of meaningful relationships with students, and technological unemployment due to AI. AI and other digital technologies change teacher practices, sometimes with unintended consequences. Southgate et al. (2019) describe teacher well-being as directly linked to children's health. The authors note, \"Teachers need time to equip themselves with the knowledge and skills required to harness [AI] technologies for deep learning and for broader social good\" (p. 14). Importantly, the relationship of AI to teaching all subjects is a significant one whereby teachers need time \"to build their knowledge about AI and emerging technologies for learning and to acquaint themselves with current interdisciplinary debates about AI and the future of work and its ethical quandaries\" (p. 14). This becomes a key point in the prolonged and developing use of AI in classrooms. \n\nUNESCO (2021) includes teachers' well-being as central to the healthy adoption of AI in education.",
