      "snippet": {
        "text": "Previous studies have focused on student-outcomes, and therefore on AI tools that foster motivation and English learning (Jeon & Lee, 2023;Kohnke et al., 2023;Marzuki et al., 2023;Mohamed, 2024;Sumakul et al., 2022;Vera, 2023). Being an EFL educator is considered a highly stressful job (as is teaching in general), with increased risk of burnout (Gregersen et al., 2021;2023;Wang, 2022). Poorer educator functioning because of stress, in turn also negatively impacts students' academic success (Wang, 2022). As such, the benefits of AI for educators are an area that deserves further attention. \n\nOverall, it seems to be the case that the perceptions of educators are developing along with AI developments. It appears the right time to start to increase the knowledge and skills of educators, starting with their most desired education in AI-based digital assistants (tutors) and their application to education and AI-based learning analytics. Further, the present study provides stimulation to encourage educators to be involved in the further development of AI and AI in education policies.",
