        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study reveals key insights into primary school English teachers continued use of AI tools, along with actionable recommendations for stakeholders. Psychological safety and trust strongly influence adoption, eachers are more likely to use AI tools when they perceive them as reliable, easy to use, and beneficial for student learning. However, anxiety about technical failures, privacy risks, and unmet expectations can deter sustained usage. Perceived risk further moderates adoption, meaning even teachers with positive attitudes may hesitate if they doubt an AI tool's stability or data security. To address these challenges, we recommend that AI developers prioritize stability, intuitive design, and transparent privacy protections to build trust. Schools and administrators should provide targeted training that addresses both technical and psychological barriers, while fostering a supportive culture for AI integration. Clear communication about security measures and institutional policies can further reduce perceived risks. For researchers, future work should examine cross-cultural and interdisciplinary differences, conduct longitudinal studies on AI's long-term impact, and develop evaluation frameworks for AI adoption interventions. By implementing these strategies, stakeholders can better support teachers in leveraging AI tools effectively, ultimately enhancing educational outcomes.",
