          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12465860, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study provides empirical evidence for the complex relationships connecting academic workload to AI adoption among preservice teachers, highlighting both direct effects and indirect pathways mediated by work stress and performance expectations. The findings demonstrate that preservice teachers actively seek technological solutions when facing excessive academic demands, both as a direct response to workload pressures and through psychological mechanisms involving stress and performance evaluations. These results emphasize the multifaceted nature of technology adoption in educational contexts, where practical necessities interact with psychological processes to shape technological behaviors. \n\nThe stronger direct effect compared with the mediated pathway suggests that immediate practical considerations may sometimes outweigh the psychological factors driving AI integration, highlighting the need for educational institutions to manage academic demands thoughtfully while providing comprehensive support for effective technology adoption. By addressing both the practical pressures and the psychological processes influencing preservice teachers' technological decisions, teacher training programs can promote more intentional and pedagogically sound integration of AI tools in educational practice. \n\nThis research contributes to our understanding of the psychosocial dynamics underlying technological adoption in educational contexts, providing valuable insights for developing more effective teacher preparation programs that recognize the complex interplay between academic demands, psychological responses, and technological behaviors. Future research should build on these findings to develop more comprehensive models of technology adoption that account for the multifaceted pressures and processes shaping educational practice in increasingly technologically mediated learning environments.",
