          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.3390/encyclopedia5010011?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.3390/encyclopedia5010011, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Goldman et al. [18] identified ways that AI supports both student-facing and teacherfacing tasks. For teacher-focused tasks, such as creating IEP snapshots to simplify complex IEPs, generative AI tools like ChatGPT can save time by generating templates or summarizing redacted content. When using any AI tool for these types of tasks, it is necessary for users to ensure compliance with privacy policies and statutes, particularly when the functions include personally identifiable data. Similarly, AI can be used to streamline the creation of parent resources, including resource lists, home strategies, and multilingual translations, reducing the workload and supporting diverse families [18]. \n\nFor student-facing tasks, special education teachers often adapt complex texts, such as grade-level novels, to meet the decoding and comprehension needs of students with reading disabilities, and they are required to monitor students' IEP goals through tailored assessments and data collection. These time-intensive processes can be significantly streamlined using generative AI, which can produce summaries, revised content at specific reading levels, and customized word lists, fluency passages, and comprehension questions. In all cases, it is necessary for a skilled professional to thoroughly review the generated materials to ensure accuracy and appropriateness. \n\nOverall, AI systems can offer transformative advantages in special education by streamlining lesson planning, enabling individualized instruction, and providing on-demand professional development, empowering teachers by saving time and enhancing their instructional practices while addressing the diverse needs of students with disabilities [11].",
        "snippetKind": "body",
