        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Classroom engagement is \"the quality of a student's connection or involvement with the endeavor of classroom and hence with the people, activities, goals, values and place that compose it\" [46]. As a multi-dimensioned construct, engagement has been conceptualized as consisting of three elements: behavioral, emotional, and cognitive engagement [46]. Behavioral engagement refers to the level on which students are actively involved in their academic tasks, exhibiting perseverance, concentration and exertion [25]. Students with high behavioral engagement participate the classroom activities actively and long for success. Emotional engagement includes the mental states experienced by students during learning activities in terms of their ardor, interests, and enjoyment, alongside their affiliation with their schools, feelings of connectedness to the schools, and establishment of meaningful relationships with their teachers and peers [25]. Students with high emotional engagement show high motivation towards learning and school. Cognitive engagement means the students' psychology in learning tasks, which includes their willingness to comprehend and master skills, as well as the cognitive processes they utilize in accomplishing tasks, such as utilizing learning approaches and tactics to grasp educational content [25]. Students with high cognitive engagement demonstrate willingness to accept challenge, solve problems flexibly and evaluate the connection of their classroom learning and their future development.",
